<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 10px;">Glossary</h2>

  <div style="margin-top: 10px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;">Cost of Attendance (COA):</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The total amount (not including grants and scholarships) that it will cost you to go to school during the 2021-22 school year. COA includes tuition and fees, housing and meals, books and supplies, transportation, loan fees, and dependent care. It also includes costs for study-abroad programs. For students attending less than half-time, the COA includes tuition and fees; an allowance for books, supplies, and transportation; and dependent care expenses.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;">Expected Family Contribution: A number used by your school to calculate the amount of federal student aid you are eligible to receive. It is based on the financial information provided in your Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA). This is not the amount of money your family will have to pay for college; it is the amount that the federal government uses to determine how much federal student aid you will receive.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;">Federal Work-Study: A federal student aid program that provides part-time employment while the student is enrolled in school to help pay his or her educational expenses. The student must seek out and apply for a work-study job. The amount of money the student earns from this job is used to pay for school costs and the amount he or she earns cannot exceed the total amount awarded by the school for the award year. The availability of work-study jobs varies by school.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;">Grants and Scholarships: Student aid funds that do not have to be repaid. Grants are often need-based, while scholarships are usually merit-based. Loans, on the other hand, must be repaid. Loans are often not need-based, while scholarships are usually merit-based. Federal loans, Federal aid from most advantageous to least advantageous, are called Direct Subsidized Loans, Direct Unsubsidized Loans, and Parent PLUS Loans. You can find more information about federal student aid at <a href="https://studentaid.gov">https://studentaid.gov</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;">Direct Subsidized Loan: Loans that the U.S. Department of Education pays the interest on while you're in school at least half-time, for the first six months after you leave school (referred to as a grace period), and during a period of deferment (a postponement of loan payments).</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;">Direct Unsubsidized Loan: Loans for which the borrower is responsible for paying the interest during all periods. If you choose not to pay the interest while you are in school and during grace periods and deferment for forbearance periods, your interest will accrue (accumulate) and be capitalized (that is, your interest will be added to the principal amount of your loan).</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;">Private Loan: A loan available to the parents of dependent undergraduate students for which the borrower is fully responsible for paying the interest and principal.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold;">Net Cost: An estimate of the actual cost that a student and his or her family need to pay in a given year to cover education expenses for the student to attend a particular school. Net price is determined by taking the institution's cost of attendance and subtracting any grants and scholarships for which the student may be eligible.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">For more information visit <a href="https://studentaid.gov">https://studentaid.gov</a>.</p>
  </div>
</div>

